Reasoning: In a toy superposition model with parent–child hierarchical features, children fire only conditional on their parent, so parent features carry strictly more activation mass and higher frequency. A Matryoshka SAE's multi-scale loss forces the smallest (25%) prefix to shoulder the bulk of reconstruction on its own, which preferentially allocates the most frequently firing features there — this is exactly the hierarchical-organization effect reported in the Matryoshka SAE literature (coarser/higher-frequency features land in earlier prefixes). So the direction of the effect (parents matched in the first 25% prefix more often than children) is the expected one.

The main risk is statistical rather than directional: with 12 seeds and a difference-of-proportions statistic, the paired 95% t interval's lower bound must clear zero. In toy planted-feature setups these effects are usually clean and highly consistent across seeds (children also risk being partially absorbed into parent latents, further widening the gap), so the decision rule is more likely than not satisfied. Still, if child frequencies are nearly equal to parent frequencies or matching saturates in the small prefix, the effect could shrink and the interval could straddle zero. Weighing a strong expected direction against moderate power, I lean yes but not overwhelmingly.

